Industrial upcycling transforms post-consumer waste streams into higher-value products through creative remanufacturing and material recombination. Unlike recycling that reduces material to base constituents, upcycling preserves material form and value while creating entirely new product categories. This strategy captures value in abundant waste streams—textiles, plastics, metals—that would otherwise require energy-intensive reprocessing. Products designed for upcycling potential incorporate materials and components valuable for secondary applications, fundamentally shifting waste perception from disposal problem to resource opportunity.